AN ORDINANCE IMPOSING AN OCCUPATION TAX ON QUALIFIED BUSINESSES AND PRACTITIONERS OF PROFESSIONS AND OCCUPATIONS WITHIN THE UNINCOPORATED AREA OF WARREN COUNTY; TO PROVIDE FOR THE ISSUANCE OF A COUNTY OCCUPATION TAX PERMIT UPON PAYMENT OF THE OCCUPATION TAX; TO PROVIDE FOR ENFORCEMENT OF THE ORDINANCE; AND FOR OTHER PURPOSES.

WHEREAS, 0O.C.G.A. §§ 48-13-5 et. seg. authorizes the Board of Commissioners (“Board”), as the governing authority for Warren County, to enact an ordinance for the levy, assessment, and collection of an occupation tax on certain businesses and practitioners of professions and occupations in the unincorporated part of the county;

WHEREAS, the Board desires to provide for the issuance of county business licenses to those business and practitioners owing and paying the required occupation tax;

WHEREAS, the Board has conducted a public hearing concerning the proposed ordinance in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 48-13-6(c);

NOW, THEREFORE, BE IT ORDAINED by the Board that the following amendments shall be made to the Code of Warren County:

11:
- (a) The following businesses and practitioners shall be excluded from occupation tax, registration fees, or regulatory fees under the provisions of this Article but shall be subject to taxation and regulation as otherwise provided by law:

- (1) Those businesses regulated by the Public Service Commission and the Department of Public Safety;

- (2) Those electrical service businesses organized under Chapter 3 of Title 46;

- (3) Any farm operation for the production from or on the land of agricultural products, but not including any agribusiness;

- (4) Any state or local authority, nonprofit organization, or vendor operating under a contract with a tax-exempt agricultural fair, as that term is defined in Code Section 2- 2-8;

- (5) Real estate brokers, real estate agents, or real estate companies whose offices, are located outside the jurisdiction and who sell property inside the jurisdiction;

- (6) Attorneys authorized to practice law by the State Bar of Georgia;

- (7) Any person whose office is maintained by and who is employed in practice exclusively by the United States, the State of Georgia, or a municipality or county of this state, or instrumentality of the United States, the State of Georgia, or a municipality or county of the state; and

- (8) Any business on which the levy of such occupational tax is prohibited or restricted by the laws of the State of Georgia or the United States including, but not limited to:

- (i) Cooperative marketing associations governed by O.C.G.A. § 2-10-105;

Gi) Insurance companies governed by O.C.G.A. § 33-8-8 et seq.;

Gii) | Motor common carriers governed by O.C.G.A. § 46-7-15;

- (iv) Those businesses governed by 0.C.G.A. § 48-5-355;

- (v) Agricultural products and livestock raised in the state of Georgia governed by O.C.G.A. § 48-5-356;

- (vi) | Depository financial institutions governed by O.C.G.A. § 48-6-93;

- (vii) Facilities operated by a charitable trust governed by O.C.G.A. § 48-13-55; and

(viii) Disabled veterans and blind persons as governed by O.C.G.A. § 43-12-1.

- (b) This Article shall not be construed to repeal other provisions of state law relating to local governments’ occupation tax, registration fees, or regulatory fees for businesses or practitioners of professions or occupations.

- (c) The Board, in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 48-13-10(, may by subsequent ordinance or resolution provide for an exemption or reduction in occupation tax to one or more types of ‘businesses or practitioners of occupations or professions as part of a plan for economic development or attracting or encouraging selected types of businesses or practitioners of selected occupations or professions. Such exemptions or reductions in occupation tax shall not be arbitrary or capricious, and the reasons shall be set forth in the minutes of the governing authority.

13:
- (a) Each business and practitioner of any profession or occupation subject to the occupation tax for the current calendar year shall, within thirty (30) days of the time of payment of such occupation tax, file with the tax commissioner, on a form furnished or otherwise approved by the tax commissioner, a signed return setting forth:

- (1) The legal name of such business and any associated trade name(s);

- (2) The mailing address for each location and office where the business, profession or occupation is conducted in the county;

- (3) The line or lines of business conducted at each such location or office and the sales and use tax identification number assigned to such business by the Department of Revenue if such business is required to have such identifying number; and

- (4) The number of employees of the business or practitioner, computed according to the guidelines set forth in this Article.

- (b) Where any business or practitioner has commenced operation of a business after January 1 of the current year, the aforementioned return shall be filed within thirty (30) days of the date of commencement of such business.

14:
- (a) The Administrator shall perform all functions necessary to interpret, administer, and enforce this Article, including collection of the tax imposed herein and summoning violators of this Article to appear in a court of competent jurisdiction;

- (b) The County Attorney is authorized to advise and assist the Administrator in performing the duties set forth in subsection (a) of this Section and to prosecute violations of this Article;

- (c) In addition to the other remedies available, the official charged with the collection of the tax or fee shall issue executions against the delinquent taxpayers for:

- (1) the amount of the taxes or fees due when the taxes or fees become due; and (2) any penalty and interest imposed by this Article.

- (d) A court of competent jurisdiction may impose a civil fine no greater than $500.00 for failure to pay the occupation tax. Such a civil fine may be enforced by the contempt power of the court,

- (e) When a nulla bona entry has been entered by proper authority upon an execution issued by the Administrator or tax commissioner against any person defaulting on the occupation tax, the person against whom the entry is made shall not be allowed or entitled to have or collect any fees or charges whatever for services rendered after the entry of the nulla bona. If, at any time after the entry of nulla bona has been made, the person against whom the execution issues pays the tax in full together with all interest and costs accrued on the tax, the person may collect any fees and charges due him or her as though he or she had never defaulted in the payment of the tax.
